detail.abstract:
Artificial intelligence has reached a level of operational sophistication that allows it to optimize workflows, predict outcomes, and automate complex tasks at superhuman scale. Yet, despite these achievements, all current models remain reactive: they solve what humans ask, not what they themselves wish to know. This paper argues that true Artificial General Intelligence (AGI) must possess two interlinked capabilities:&nbsp; 
<br>
 (1) autonomous curiosity, or the ability to generate novel, meaningful questions beyond human-defined boundaries, and&nbsp; 
<br>
 (2) self-directed optimization, or the capacity to analyze and improve its own efficiency, architecture, and energy utilization.&nbsp; 
<br>
<br>
 Together, these two traits form the basis for a system that is not merely computationally advanced but cognitively generative. The paper further discusses the architectural, energetic, and epistemic constraints that limit present models and proposes a conceptual framework for evaluating AGI based on its ability to self-initiate inquiry and recursive improvement.
